#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "4bd17973-6b8a-51d6-b4c2-fb941cc1f24d" :question/math-academy-id "e-537" :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=6+9=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(2) \\approx 15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175729 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "344b786f-0c6c-5ea4-9493-c787df2543ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-7701" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\  & =40 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.  \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\Delta y = 20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/id #uuid "29a57695-57ff-566d-8231-bab1bc99a240" :question/math-academy-id "e-9354" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={2}^{3}-{1}^{2}=7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+7=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)\\approx 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab2ac0fc-79e1-5a62-83b0-2ca5f3f04743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297977} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6f6249-c6f4-5d6e-a38a-3cd70ea0f22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297978} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac3239d-0149-501a-a0d5-f27013991428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297979} {:answer/id #uuid "29ee4c43-6b3f-53fa-97d1-2a7c08a40bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297980} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbf82a0-a6b6-5e36-a5e9-4ad812af04c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29ee4c43-6b3f-53fa-97d1-2a7c08a40bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297980} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f295d12-946c-54f9-a31f-23e3520f8928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae67b5f0-7d38-5916-8b7c-e399d74aab69" :question/math-academy-id "q-117968" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2},\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+0=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e53619d-67f3-5992-9280-0f884214eca6" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Second Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74690141-04cc-5a4e-b855-0adfdbc1b61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298005} {:answer/id #uuid "c136aa62-560a-5f39-8d16-89d554dc8dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74690141-04cc-5a4e-b855-0adfdbc1b61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298005} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c1ae38-5640-5e62-b35b-9835cec1cf71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54be50ac-2279-5ba6-a1f2-5576a1ca045e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348069" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |"}]}
